Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579 align:start position:0%
 
this morning Attorney General Jeff  Sessions will announce new government  tools for the opioid epidemic just this  week New Jersey Governor Chris Christie  who chairs the White House opioid abuse  Commission says the government isn't  putting nearly enough money into the  fight President Trump declared the  epidemic a public health emergency last  month but critics complained that didn't  go far enough because it didn't bring  additional funding in our ongoing series  issues that matter we're taking a closer  look at the opioid epidemic it has taken  more than half a million lives since the  year 2000 retired Navy Admiral sandy  Winnefeld CBS News military and homeland  security analyst is with us at the table  for him this epidemic is very personal  and very painful too he lost his 19 year  old son Jonathan in September to a  fentanyl laced batch of heroin  Jonathan struggled with addiction for  several years and the 1fl family is now  dedicated to studying this problem in  saving other families from the same  heartache Admiral good morning your wife  marries with you this morning in the  green room and she says it's as close to  hell as you can get and the article that  was just released and the Atlantic I  think is so powerful and so personal  because you say that for most people  it's a simple understanding of what we  think opioid addiction is you think it  happens to dysfunctional families with  dysfunctional people and you all were  certainly not that know this this runs  the gamut rich poor black white male  female young old it's impacting every  element of our society and I think the  public is beginning to become aware that  there is an epidemic we need to do a lot  better job of understanding the exact  nature of the epidemic and what it's  going to take - let's talk about  Jonathan because you dropped him off at  school three days later you get the  phone call you guys thought everything  was okay he was getting better tell us a  little bit about his history so John was  a grip is a really good kid well-liked  by all of us teammates his friends and  the like but he grew up with anxiety and  I was mixed misdiagnosed as attention  deficit he was prescribed adderall which  was exactly the wrong thing to do and  that started him down the road to self  medication one of three what I would  call gateway entry points into addiction  one of which would be a physical injury  that gets over prescribed another is  self medication and the third disorder  the party entry John went through 15  months of  inpatient train therapy and did a really  good job he got his emergency medical  technician qualification he was  bright-eyed bushy-tailed ready to go to  school but that that molecule had  burrowed itself deeply into his brain  three days later after dropping him off  you get a call right the worst most  shocking call of my life  we thought he was on such an upward  trajectory he was really excited to be  at school excited to be having his  emergency medical technician  qualification and it fooled us you know  it really fooled us Admiral I'm I'm you  know thank you for your service and all  that you and Mary have done for this  country and then also for sharing this  personal this personal struggle because  I know you're trying to continue your  services country and the people to help  you know one of the interesting things I  found too about your beautiful son  Jonathan too  is this what we're learning about the  brain right and that really struck me  about how highly susceptible the brain  is to dependency and you wrote about  those gateways you talked about him  being prescribed adderall and then using  alcohol to come down from the adderall  and then marijuana what do we know about  marijuana in the brain well there's an  awful lot of research going on right now  about that and there's a certainly  associated a lot of controversies with  that but everything that I've come to  understand is that marijuana doesn't  necessarily cause opioid addiction but  it makes the brain more susceptible to  it more welcoming to the morphine  molecule that burrows itself deeply into  the brain so I think you know they're  important medical uses for both opioids  and marijuana so we need to be cautious  with this but I do think it makes sense  to re-examine the legal age for  marijuana it's much more potent than  when we were growing up as kids we just  we it it really warrants a lot of  research into the impact that it's  having especially on young people so  even if you can get off you whether it's  the alcohol or the drugs that you're  using the brain is constantly craving it  is a lifelong battle and the brain  literally changes and we were able to  watch over 15 months while Jonathan's  brain recovered he turned back into our  son it was remarkable to see he went out  and got his emergency medical technician  qualification  was you know sort of you know touchdown  this kid is back but you said you were  you guys were blinded by the optimism  that you missed the signs of the relapse  what did you miss well we we took him up  to the mountains to a house for the  weekend before he went into school and  he was Restless and sweating and shifty  and we just attributed it to maybe too  high of a dose of medication for his  anxiety and also his nervousness about  going to school because you've just  written this powerful essay about  wanting to help people who had the same  situation he had and when in fact he was  probably experiencing symptoms of  withdrawal because he hadn't had the  drug in a few days because he was with  us and tell us why you think this is a  national security issue it's a national  security issue for a couple of reasons  one cost right there was there's  tremendous medical costs associated with  this there's tremendous loss of  productivity and in the workplace the  original estimate was around 78 billion  dollar a year cost the United States the  White House just recently revised that  to over five hundred billion dollars a  year that sounds to me like it's more  than a public health emergency it's  actually a national emergency and so I  think it's something that is really a  national emergency and needs to be  treated as such needs to be funded as  such and we need to have some very  thoughtful approaches to how we tackle  the problem all of which are very  interrelated you can't do one without  the other or you fail you know Admiral  your piece in the Atlantic was  heartbreaking and I'm struck by what you  wrote about how difficult it was to find  adequate treatment for Jonathan how is  addiction treated in this country what  you learn well there there are a host of  treatment centers all over the country  they all do different things they are  all maybe specialized in one particular  thing the hardest thing for us was to  find a place that would treat the dual  diagnosis of anxiety some sort of mental  illness and addiction those are two  things that have to be treated together  very carefully not every facility does  that and unfortunately our case the  military medical system wouldn't pay for  that how are you able to do this sandy  because this happened in September you  guys just had to go through Thanksgiving  without him christmas is coming how are  you able and why are you doing it a  couple of things you know this is part  of our recovery Jonathan in  this powerful essay he wrote as an  incoming freshman at Denver University  said he wanted to help other people with  this problem so we want to carry his  mission forward if we can we have the  ability to do that we have some very  generous remarkable friends who have  helped us both with the intellectual  piece of this and with the financial  piece of this and we just started a  website up this morning that's called  safe project not us it's in its  embryonic form you won't make a  difference you they were telling me guys  in the green room that Mike Morell and  his wife were one of the first people  that came to you came to your side - I  was where I was rushing back from Boston  to be with Mary and Michael and Mary  Beth generously came right over and took  care Mary we really need friends at a  time like that thank you very much thank  you thank you Sam yes for highlighting  and I you know we have a tradition in  the military of challenge coins okay  instead of doing a prayer card at  Jonathan's funeral we did a challenge  coin for Jonathan I'd like for each of  you - thank you highlighting it thank  you very much take a look at that yeah  thank you so much I know we appreciate  it

